Screen Sharing

Share a browser tab or window directly with other people, with no account, no install and no server of your own. The whole app is a single HTML file.

Live site: https://cross-creative.github.io/easy_screen/

How it works

Video travels directly from the host's browser to each viewer's browser (peer-to-peer, using WebRTC). A public [PeerJS](https://peerjs.com) server is used only to help browsers find each other. No video passes through it, and nothing is stored.

Quick start

Sharing

1. Open the site on a computer.
2. Pick a quality level and choose whether to share audio.
3. Click Choose a tab or window and select what to share.
4. Send the 6-character room code, or the link, to your viewers.

Watching

1. Open the link, or type the room code on the home page and click Join.
2. If the video starts muted because of browser autoplay rules, click Unmute.

Features

- Room codes: each host gets a random 6-character code, plus a shareable link that joins the room directly
- Tab or window sharing with optional audio
- Quality levels from 480p up to 4K, adjustable while streaming
- Warning for the host when upload bandwidth or CPU is overloaded, with a one-click option to lower the quality
- Estimated upload usage and a live viewer counter
- Stop and resume sharing, or switch to another tab or window, without kicking anyone out of the room
- Options panel for the host, with a confirmation before ending the room
- One tab per viewer per room: duplicate tabs from the same browser are blocked
- Volume and mute controls for viewers, audio toggle for the host
- Fullscreen for both host and viewers
- English and Portuguese (Brazil), detected from the browser and remembered
- Light and dark theme, following the system setting

 Quality levels and upload

Each viewer receives a separate copy of the video, so the host's upload grows with the audience. These are approximate values per viewer:

| Level | Resolution | Frame rate | Upload per viewer |
|---|---|---|---|
| Very light | 480p | 10 fps | ~0.3 Mbps |
| Economy | 720p | 15 fps | ~0.8 Mbps |
| Balanced | 720p | 24 fps | ~1.5 Mbps |
| High | 1080p | 30 fps | ~3 Mbps |
| QHD | 1440p | 60 fps | ~8 Mbps |
| 4K | 2160p | 30 fps | ~15 Mbps |

Example: 4 viewers at Balanced need about 6 Mbps of upload. The actual resolution also depends on the size of the shared tab or window.

Browser support

| | Share | Watch |
|---|---|---|
| Desktop Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari | Yes | Yes |
| Android browsers | No | Yes |
| iOS browsers | No | Yes |

Mobile browsers do not currently support screen capture, so on phones the site only lets you watch. Audio capture also depends on the browser: sharing a tab's audio works best in Chrome and Edge, and sharing a whole window's audio is limited.

Deploy your own copy

Only `index.html` is required.

GitHub Pages

1. Put `index.html` in the root of a repository.
2. In the repository settings, open Pages and select the branch to publish.

Netlify

Drag the folder containing `index.html` into [app.netlify.com/drop](https://app.netlify.com/drop).

Both serve the site over HTTPS, which browsers require for screen capture.

Limitations

- Mesh topology: the host uploads one stream per viewer. It is meant for small groups, roughly 3 to 6 people.
- Depends on the free public PeerJS server, which can be slow or unavailable.
- Viewers behind very restrictive networks, such as some school or company networks, may fail to connect without a TURN server.
- Duplicate-tab detection only works within the same browser profile. Another browser, a private window or another device counts as a different viewer.

Privacy

- No accounts, no analytics and no cookies.
- The browser stores only your language choice and a random identifier used to detect duplicate tabs. Both stay on your device.
- Because connections are peer-to-peer, the host and each viewer can see each other's PUBLIC IP address. Keep this in mind before sharing a room code publicly.
- Only share content you have the right to share. Streaming protected material, such as movies or series, may violate the terms of the service that provides it.

